 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "In Chef Barinov's office, Lyova tries out the next chapter of his psychology audiobook by copying every single gesture, facial expression, and posture his boss makes.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Копируя мимику, вы получаете контроль.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты представляешь, что Герман придумал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Он живую змею нам подкинул!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Что же надо с ним сделать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "А ничего не делать, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Он будет ждать гадости от вас.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Нет, это слишком просто для него.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Совсем не просто, дорогой шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты зачем сейчас это делаешь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "А что я такого делаю?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты меня очень сильно нервируешь!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ну не нервничайте так, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты что, издеваешься надо мной?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Да уходи ты уже отсюда!",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Let's examine each line as Lyova's psychological mirroring backfires.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Копируя мимику, вы получаете контроль.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'By copying facial expressions, you gain control.' The verbal adverb ending in '-ya' means 'while copying' or 'by copying'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты представляешь, что Герман придумал?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Can you imagine what German thought up?' Asking 'do you imagine?' is the natural spoken way to say 'can you believe...?'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Он живую змею нам подкинул!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He planted a live snake on us!' The prefix 'pod-' on 'threw' means to secretly plant or slip something in.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Что же надо с ним сделать?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What should we do to him?' In Russian, doing something 'to him' uses the preposition 'with' plus the instrumental case.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "А ничего не делать, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Do nothing at all, chef.' Using the infinitive echoes the exact grammar of Barinov's question.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Он будет ждать гадости от вас.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He will be waiting for a dirty trick from you.' The verb 'to wait' takes the genitive case when waiting for a general thing like trouble.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Нет, это слишком просто для него.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, that is too simple for him.' Barinov rejects the peaceful advice with the degree adverb 'too'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Совсем не просто, дорогой шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Not simple at all, dear chef.' Lyova strokes his moustache in the exact same rhythm as Barinov.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты зачем сейчас это делаешь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why are you doing that right now?' Barinov notices that Lyova is mirroring his every move.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "А что я такого делаю?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what am I doing?' Lyova blinks innocently while copying Barinov's stance.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты меня очень сильно нервируешь!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You are getting on my nerves really badly!' The transitive verb means 'you are making me nervous'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Ну не нервничайте так, шеф.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, do not get so worked up, chef.' Notice the pair: 'nervirovat' means to make someone else nervous, while 'nervnichat' means to feel nervous yourself.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты что, издеваешься надо мной?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What, are you mocking me?' The verb 'to mock/taunt' governs the preposition 'nad' ('over') plus the instrumental case.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Да уходи ты уже отсюда!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Just get out of here already!' Losing his temper completely, Barinov chases his sous-chef out of the office. Now listen to the whole office scene again at natural speed.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "lyova",
   "t": "Копируя мимику, вы получаете контроль.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "anya",
   "t": "Ты представляешь, что Герман придумал?",
   "gap": 0.5
